Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
the russian invasion of ukraine has led  to a radical departure in german foreign  and security policy chancellor schultz  has pledged to increase defense spending  and he's also ditched a gas pipeline  project with russia a project that  former chancellor merkel refused to  touch these changes have been welcomed  by washington where just several weeks  ago lawmakers were openly  questioning germany as a reliable  partner  today germany's new defense minister  christine lambrecht visited the pentagon  in washington here's u.s defense  secretary lloyd austin speaking earlier  today  now germany has shown tremendous  leadership at this crucial moment  your chancellor's decisions to bolster  germany's armed forces are bold and  historic  and we look forward to working with you  to implement these important changes  we also salute germany's decision to  send security assistance to ukraine  your example helped to inspire other  allies and partners to follow  well my next guest tonight is matthew  karknichnik he is the chief europe  correspondent for politico here in  berlin matthew it's good to have you on  the program you wrote  i dare say a blistering piece about  german politicians in russia this week  but the headline putin's useful german  idiots and you write it shouldn't  surprise anyone that berlin spent the  past 16 years with its feet firmly  planted on the wrong side of the divide  over how to handle russia  explain to our viewers what you mean  there  well i mean that it's been clear to many  people in europe and in the united  states for quite a long time that putin  shouldn't be trusted  but germany ignored all of these  warnings uh consistently and decided  instead to do what german industry  wanted and what made sense for them from  a  energy point of view in terms of  getting cheap gas from russia to heat  german households and more importantly  even for  german industry and it ignored all of  these warnings and it worked well in the  short term  but it was a very  uh sort of myopic view i think in terms  of what russia was was really up to  you go down the list of political  parties and politicians in your article  you also call out  angela merkel for being the leader of  this disastrous policy i mean she was  often called the putin whisperer she was  considered by some even former u.s  president barack obama to be like an  oracle with a window into the kremlin  but you say that she has earned a place  in the pantheon of political naivete  alongside neville chamberlain  was angela merkel the putin whisperer  really the putin appeaser  she was definitely the the putin  appeaser she might have been whispering  to him but he certainly wasn't uh wasn't  listening and i think a lot of us looked  at her record even before this on  foreign policy and said that you know  it's not everything that a lot of people  in western capitals think it was uh not  just in terms of russia but in terms of  her policies towards towards europe uh  the greek crisis and and other issues so  uh you know i think it's sort of  interesting that you're seeing  the miracle legacy crumble  so so quickly but there can be no doubt  that this was a historic error that she  made and it goes back to the decision in  2008 in bucharest at the nato summit  when germany resisted including ukraine  and georgia in nato  and at the time the united states under  george w bush was pushing her in the  other direction she stood her ground  and eventually got the french to go  along with her and this was the  beginning of a process that has  continued up until a couple of weeks ago  of basically forgiving the russians for  every misdeed looking the other way and  continuing to pursue these gas deals and  the problem i think in a lot of people's  perception is because of the role  gerhard schroeder her predecessor as  chancellor played in all of this there's  been a lot of focus on his role and he  of course  joined as chairman of north stream two  uh the the pipeline project that has  been so controversial between russia and  germany but uh really the power rested  with merkel and the people people around  her including frankfurt steinmeier who  at the time was foreign minister from  her coalition partner the social  democrats and is now of course german  president  you highlight um in your article what is  an almost  self-righteous holier-than-thou attitude  by the political elite here in berlin  when you describe what the german  parliament did the day that ukrainian  president zielinski spoke  i mean he delivered a scathing  indictment of german complicity in the  creation of the putin war machine  what happened  directly after that speech in the  bundestag  well directly afterwards the mps got up  and gave him a standing ovation which  was somewhat bizarre given how critical  he had had been of them and then they  just moved on to business as usual  and congratulated a couple of mps for  their on their birthdays that day  and just went on to other matters and  didn't really have anything like a a a  real discussion about what they just  heard from  zielinski and some of the people in the  room in the opposition said  this was sort of the the most  embarrassing day for the bundestag in in  living memory  you know matthew before we run out of  time you know i read your article and i  kept thinking this is exactly what we  have reported  time and time again for the past five  years or so especially over nordstrom 2  and yet  many times it has felt like that there  was no resonance or not even an echo in  the german media  why do you think that is  well i think there's been a tendency in  germany for for decades really in their  relationships with the rest of the world  and in particular with the relationship  with russia to wrap everything in this  sort of moral packaging and say that you  know germany has this historic debt  towards russia and this is why we need  to pursue dialogue as they call it with  russia no matter what happens in reality  something else is going on here this was  a purely mercantilistic policy  that benefited the german economy that  benefited big german industry and uh you  know trying to sell it off as this very  moralistic position i think has put  germany now  in a extremely tough spot because they  would like to just sort of say well  we're going to kind of move on from this  we were wrong  um  you know  as they say in in german they're just  going to wipe the slate clean and and  forget that this happened and i don't  think that uh their allies in europe or  across the atlantic are going to be  willing to let them do that yeah they're  going to need a really big schwarm  that's for sure matthew karnichnik chief  europe correspondent for politico here  in berlin matthew we appreciate your  time tonight thank you  thank you  lombrecht stressed that in light of  russia's aggression germany and its  allies stand closer together than ever  before  putin did not manage to divide nato or  the eu  we stand together and we are reliable  partners  our allies on nato's eastern flank in  particular can rely on this without  question  we support ukraine with arms deliveries  in their incredible fight against a  brutal aggressor  and we in germany will invest in our  security  and nato security  height  will receive an additional 100 billion  euros for this  this is an important contribution and it  was good to hear that it is greatly  appreciated here in washington in  washington  and for more now i want to bring in  thomas silverhorn he's a member of the  german parliament for the conservative  csu party he's also the go-to person for  transatlantic relations mr silberhorn we  appreciate you taking the time to talk  with us  tonight before the invasion  many in washington  had doubts about germany being a  reliable partner when it comes to russia  what do you say to them now five weeks  into this war  germany has always been a reliable  partner in our nato alliance  new is the message that also the new  government completely sticks to the two  percent goal of nato this has not been  the case before it has not been  explicitly laid down in the coalition  treaty on the contrary the green party  explicitly denied to support the two  percent goal this indeed has changed and  this is a very good message for the  german army and for the nato  mr silverhorn there are critics who say  that germany has been on the wrong side  of history for decades with its russia  policy and that it took a war  to make  german politicians see the light  what do you say  the lesson we all have to learn in  particular in europe and in germany is  there is no appeasement that can be  successful  we have to  clearly distinct  between liberal democracies in the west  and autocratic systems like russia and  china there is a systemic gap that  cannot be overruled by which treaty ever  yes we need coordination and cooperation  where this is possible with russia or  with china but there is and there will  always be confrontation in the  substantial issue of the systemic  rivalry between democracies and  autocratic age yeah you mentioned  appeasement  many in washington say it was a  catastrophic mistake  for germany to allow the nordstream 2  pipeline to be constructed you know  former chancellor angela merkel she  refused to say no to the pipeline  was it a mistake that you're now having  to correct  well it has now been corrected and yes  indeed germany is to a large extent  dependent from gas and oil supply from  russia  similar volumes are delivered to the  united states as you know so there is a  variety of arguments on the table but  now things have been clarified after  this brutal attack of russia against  ukraine we have to be aware of the  substantial  preconditions of our bilateral  relations and yes we have to stand at  the right side and this is at the side  of western democracies this is clearer  than ever  i know that there are a lot of people in  washington particularly at the pentagon  who are glad to hear that germany is  increasing its defense spending at the  same time  the head of nato's supreme command for  europe told u.s senators this week that  he expects there to be a permanent  increase in the  number of u.s troops needed to defend  europe against russia  what role is germany going to play here  is germany going to be able to punch  at its weight or even above militarily  in the future  an additional 100 billion euros is a  huge step forward for the german army  for this current legislative period but  it should not lead to the  misunderstanding that you get  a combat ready army by 100 billion euros  only this is again a huge step for the  next few years but much much more is  needed for the current period of nato  and eu planning until 2031.  so yes it's a good message but  government will never be done it's a  permanent task we have to fulfill and  that's why the nato goal of two percent  of our gdp for defense is so crucial and  is much more important than this volume  of 100 billion additional billion for  the next two years  mr thomas silverhorn we appreciate you  taking the time to talk with us tonight  valuable insight into transatlantic  relations at the moment thank you  thank you

